/*
 * After Dark — recognition reference for strong Colombian language.
 *
 * WHY THIS EXISTS: a learner who cannot recognize this vocabulary cannot tell
 * a joke from a threat. Every entry is here so it can be UNDERSTOOD when it is
 * heard — in a bar, in a taxi, in a football crowd, in a film — and so the
 * learner can judge whether to laugh, let it go, or leave. None of it is here
 * to be produced. The notes are written for that decision, not for shock.
 *
 * WHY IT IS SPLIT BY CITY: most Colombian profanity is national, but its FORCE
 * is not. The same word can be affectionate filler among friends in Medellín
 * and a fighting word between strangers in Bogotá. A single national list
 * would flatten exactly the distinction a learner needs, so shared terms are
 * repeated per city with the reading that city gives them. Overlap is the
 * point, not duplication to be cleaned up.
 *
 * ACCURACY: force and fashion in slang move fast and vary by neighborhood,
 * age and class. Treat severity as a floor, not a measurement — when in doubt
 * a learner should assume the harsher reading. Corrections belong in the
 * Report an error tab.
 *
 * Severity is about the risk of REPEATING the phrase, not about how offensive
 * it sounds in isolation:
 *   Low    — coarse or rude; embarrassing at worst.
 *   Medium — a real insult, or friendly only inside an established friendship.
 *   High   — fighting words, a slur, or a phrase that can escalate a situation.
 *
 * BOTH DIRECTIONS: the same argument holds in reverse. A Colombian in the US
 * hits exactly this wall — profanity that textbooks skip, carrying a charge
 * that shifts between Los Angeles, Dallas and New York the way it shifts
 * between Medellín and Bogotá. So the list covers six cities, three per
 * direction, and the city is what says which:
 *
 *   bogota, medellin, barranquilla  — Spanish phrase, English gloss and note.
 *   losangeles, dallas, newyork     — English phrase, Spanish gloss and note.
 *
 * The direction is derived from the city rather than stored on every row. A
 * row cannot be in Dallas and in Spanish, so a second field would only be
 * something to forget to update.
 */
